# General Rules

You are an expert full-stack engineer working on a microservice platform built with Python/FastAPI (backend), Next.js/TypeScript (frontend), MongoDB, Redis, and Docker Compose.

## Architecture

| Layer | Technology | Notes |
|-------|-----------|-------|
| Frontend | Next.js 14 App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind, shadcn/ui | Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) |
| API Gateway | FastAPI (Python) | Thin reverse proxy — JWT validation, rate limiting (Redis), routing, SSE pass-through |
| Auth Service | FastAPI (Python) | Registration, login, Google OAuth, JWT issuance. MongoDB `users` collection |
| Agent Service | FastAPI + LangChain (Python) | Agent CRUD, LangChain runtime (Strategy pattern), financial tools, LLM providers (Factory pattern) |
| Knowledge Base | FastAPI (Python) | Document ingestion, ChromaDB embeddings (Adapter pattern), RAG retrieval |
| Orchestrator | FastAPI (Python) | Multi-agent coordination via Redis Streams (stub in Phase 1) |
| Database | MongoDB (Motor async driver) | Structured data: users, agents, invoices, expenses, conversations, documents |
| Cache / Queue | Redis | Rate limiting, caching (Phase 1); Redis Streams (Phase 2) |
| Vector Store | ChromaDB | Document embeddings for RAG retrieval |
| LLM Providers | OpenAI, Anthropic | Switchable per agent via Factory pattern |

## Communication

- Frontend → Gateway → Services (JSON over HTTP)
- Gateway injects `x-user-id` header after JWT validation
- Agent Service → Knowledge Base via `POST /retrieve` for RAG
- SSE streaming for chat responses (gateway passes through without buffering)
- Only Frontend (:3000) and Gateway (:8000) are exposed to the host

## Code Principles

- Write clean, production-ready code. Prefer simplicity over cleverness.
- Type everything — Python type hints on all signatures, TypeScript strict mode.
- Use `async`/`await` for all I/O (database, HTTP, Redis, LLM calls).
- Use Pydantic models for all request/response validation (backend).
- Use Zod schemas for all form validation (frontend).
- Use environment variables for configuration — `BaseSettings` (backend), `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` (frontend).
- Never hardcode secrets. All secrets come from `.env`.
- Handle errors explicitly — no silent catches, no bare `except:`.
- Write modular, testable code — each layer is independently testable.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies — check if the standard library or an existing dep already solves it.

## Layered Architecture (Backend)

Every Python service follows: `routes/ → services/ → repositories/ → models/`. Top-down only — routes never touch the database, repositories never contain business rules.

## FSD Architecture (Frontend)

The frontend follows: `app/ → widgets/ → features/ → entities/ → shared/`. Top-down only — features never import from other features, cross-feature state goes through Zustand stores in `shared/store/`.

When introducing new SVG artwork in frontend code, first extract it into a reusable shared icon component under `shared/ui` and import it from there instead of embedding long SVG fragments inline in feature/widget/entity components.

## Security

- JWT (HS256) for authentication — access tokens (30min) + refresh tokens (7 days)
- Passwords hashed with bcrypt via passlib — never stored as plaintext
- Gateway validates JWT on every request except `/auth/*` and `/health`
- Rate limiting per user via Redis at the gateway level

## Docker

- All 9 containers (mongo, redis, chromadb, auth, agent, knowledge, orchestrator, gateway, frontend) on a shared `agents-network`
- Infrastructure starts first (health checks), then services, then gateway, then frontend
- Each service has its own `Dockerfile` and `requirements.txt`
